WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.375
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Managing a city

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:04.950
requires lots of infrastructure,
both physical and technical.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.290
Join us in historic
downtown Austin, Texas,

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.050
as we talked to SADA systems
about their dotMaps solution

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.310
for managing projects.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.078
John, thanks so much
for joining us today.

00:00:17.078 --> 00:00:18.620
Tell us a little
bit more about SADA.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.660
JOHN BLESAK: Yeah,
so SADA kind of

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.270
started with a really
kind of basic partnership,

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.893
with Google--

00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:26.310
let's say upwards
of 10 years ago,

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:27.770
maybe even a little bit more--

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.460
kind of being one of their
trusted resellers for Google

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.610
Maps and G Suite and then
ultimately Google Cloud.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.490
I think we've become one of
their kind of go-to services

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.980
partners in the space,
where they can kind of rely

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.730
on SADA to help bring an
implementation of, like I said,

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:48.740
kind of Maps, G Suite on Google
Cloud from start to finish.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.920
Obviously, Google gives these
great tools for companies

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.200
to build products
and build workflows.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.450
But they don't necessarily
get into the services world

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.050
with it.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.000
So they need these
trusted partners, I think,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.690
that have a lot of experience
and developers and engineers

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:06.350
that Google can trust to help
bring those projects, again,

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.083
from start to finish and
allow for a proper rollout

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:10.250
and a proper implementation.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.300
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Tell
me a little bit more

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.712
about what dotMaps does.

00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:14.170
What are the selling
points for it?

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.670
JOHN BLESAK: Yeah, so
dotMaps is focused--

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.470
it's kind of a more
of a niche product

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.050
where it kind of has a
specific need within the city.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.510
Most of these cities,
what they have is they

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.790
need to coordinate capital
improvement projects that

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.200
are internal to the city.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.560
So those are repairs
to streets and roads

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.380
and things like that--
signs and signals.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.990
And then they also have to
coordinate those internal city

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.690
projects with outside
utility groups,

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.500
such as gas and electric,
water, and things like that.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.765
The biggest problem these
cities have is basically

00:01:45.765 --> 00:01:47.390
aligning with those
utility groups that

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.340
are on the outside
of the coordination

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.670
efforts within the city.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.000
And I think giving an
application that really

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
incentivizes these groups to
work together and collaborate

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.270
on an easy-to-use platform is
why we're seeing such great ROI

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.455
numbers with dotMaps.

00:02:00.455 --> 00:02:02.603
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
does dotMaps actually do?

00:02:02.603 --> 00:02:04.520
Like, what does it involve
when people use it?

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.275
JOHN BLESAK: So,
really, at its core,

00:02:06.275 --> 00:02:08.318
dotMaps is going to be
conflict resolution.

00:02:08.318 --> 00:02:09.860
So really, what you
have right now is

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.290
you've got a city that needs
to dig up First Street,

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:14.540
and they have a major project.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.430
But you also have these
other outside groups

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.290
that need to do work in the
city and the right of way

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.480
in addition to the city.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.670
So being able to
properly coordinate

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.455
that work between the city
and share materials and costs

00:02:26.455 --> 00:02:28.580
and make sure that you
don't have groups showing up

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.285
to a construction site at the
same day at the same time.

00:02:31.285 --> 00:02:32.660
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So this is kind

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.730
of like a tool that
helps centralize

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.150
all of those different things
going on at once to communicate

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.050
them better.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.547
How has the reception been
for the tool besides just ROI?

00:02:41.547 --> 00:02:43.130
JOHN BLESAK: Reception
has been great.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.810
Again, I think the adoption
rates speak volumes.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.980
I think in DOT, your adoption
rates are everything.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.470
If you can't get people
excited and on board

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.300
properly to these
softwares, they

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.340
tend to end up in kind
of the IT graveyard,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.600
I kind of call it, within DOT.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.850
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What makes
the DOT and public sector

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:04.465
as a whole different from the
rest of, well, private sector?

00:03:04.465 --> 00:03:08.570
JOHN BLESAK: I would say just
their willingness to take risks

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:09.380
with some software.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.005
Like I was saying,
I think that they've

00:03:11.005 --> 00:03:12.710
been kind of duped
to some extent

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:15.140
by some vendors over
the last couple decades

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.370
when it comes to software.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.320
And I think that
they really want

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.763
to mitigate risk as
much as possible,

00:03:20.763 --> 00:03:23.180
especially when you're using
taxpayer money and government

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.560
money to be buying these
pieces of software.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.450
So if you can get in there
and show that you can actually

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.970
do what you're saying and
prove that out and then

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.730
really generate some
real ROI numbers,

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.160
I don't see there's any
reason why the DOT wouldn't

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:38.342
continue to work with you.

00:03:38.342 --> 00:03:39.800
But it's all about
proving that you

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.020
can do what you're saying
that your product can

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.910
do before the city, I think,
trusts you enough to say,

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.910
let's go to some of these
more robust use cases,

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:48.932
these more high dollar
infrastructure projects--

00:03:48.932 --> 00:03:49.640
things like that.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.600
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
that's a big reason

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.420
that dotMaps has
been so successful.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:53.420
JOHN BLESAK: Absolutely.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.460
I mean, I think, again,
it's all about the ease

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:56.300
of use of dotMaps.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.590
It's a very, again,
niche product.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.190
And I think there's not a lot
of players that are doing it

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.640
as well as we are.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.470
And if you can get in there, and
you can see big wins in cities,

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.380
like Chicago and Seattle,
you can prove out

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.938
that you can work with any
size city at that point--

00:04:11.938 --> 00:04:13.480
cities with a lot
of one-way streets,

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.200
cities with a ton
of construction,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.110
cities with tons of cranes
and construction management

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:19.012
going on.

00:04:19.012 --> 00:04:20.720
Those are big use
cases for these cities.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.940
And there's millions
of dollars at stake

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.520
and then making sure that
you get these projects done,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:26.748
coordinated.

00:04:26.748 --> 00:04:28.790
And then really communicate
it out to the public,

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:30.308
as well, I think is crucial.

00:04:30.308 --> 00:04:31.850
You know, the
government always wants

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:35.060
to try and be transparent to
some extent with the public

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:36.882
and let them know
what's going on.

00:04:36.882 --> 00:04:38.840
That's why with dotMaps,
we actually rolled out

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.250
a public portal as well.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:44.360
So there's a very easy to
use Google-based map type

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.900
public portal where anybody
that's living in that city

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.080
can go in and actually
subscribe to their regions,

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.330
figure out when construction
is going to be happening.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.310
If I'm a business
owner, I can also

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:57.800
set up alerts and emails
that will come through.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.530
So if roads or the
right of way is

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.543
going to be shut down
in front of my business,

00:05:01.543 --> 00:05:02.460
I'd like to know that.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.862
So those are just some of
the ways that we're being--

00:05:04.862 --> 00:05:06.320
or helping, I guess,
the government

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.670
be more transparent
about what they're doing.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.090
And, again, just kind of
keep that flow of information

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.717
going from, not only the DOT,
but also to the public as well.

00:05:13.717 --> 00:05:14.800
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Awesome.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.840
Well, John, thank you so much
for coming in and telling us

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.760
more about public
sector in the cloud.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.427
JOHN BLESAK: Yeah,
appreciate your time.

00:05:20.427 --> 00:05:21.297
Thanks so much.

00:05:21.297 --> 00:05:22.880
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Thanks for watching,

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:24.850
and stay tuned as we
continue to talk with SADA

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.100
about public sector solutions.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

